        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Does a Gravel Driveway Actually Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gravel driveway prices depend mostly on one question: is there already a good base? If you have a compacted gravel or soil base that drains and holds up a car, you are buying a few inches of new surface stone and some grading, and the total often lands around $1 to $3 per square foot in most US markets. If you are starting from lawn, soft clay or a muddy track, the job includes digging out topsoil, laying fabric, building a layered base and compacting it, and the price climbs to roughly $2 to $5 per square foot or more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stone itself is cheap. Common driveway stone such as crusher run or #57 typically runs roughly $20 to $60 per ton at the quarry or supply yard, depending on the region and how far the stone has to travel. Delivery is often a separate charge, commonly somewhere around $50 to $150 or more per truckload depending on distance. Decorative stone and pea gravel cost more per ton and are usually worse for driving on, which we explain below. As with every price on this page, treat these as typical ranges that change by region and year, not quotes.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1-3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per sq ft, resurfacing</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$2-5+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per sq ft, new with base</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$20-60</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per ton, common stone</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~1.4 tons</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per cubic yard of crushed stone</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Labor and equipment are where quotes diverge. A contractor with a skid steer, a dump truck and a plate compactor or roller can build a driveway in a day or two, and you are paying for that equipment time. Excavation, hauling away topsoil, and installing a culvert where the driveway crosses a ditch can each add hundreds to a few thousand dollars. A small resurfacing job may carry a minimum charge that makes the per-foot price look high.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the short answer: budget roughly $1 to $3 per square foot for new stone on an existing base, roughly $2 to $5 or more for a new build, and a few hundred dollars every couple of years for upkeep. That still makes gravel the cheapest driveway to install by a wide margin. The rest of this guide covers which stone to buy, how much of it you need, what pushes the price up, and when it is worth paying more for asphalt or concrete.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Much Does It Cost by Driveway Size?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The table below shows rough installed ranges for common driveway sizes. The low column assumes fresh stone over a sound existing base; the high column assumes a new driveway with excavation, fabric and a layered base on reasonably cooperative soil. Wet ground, long hauls, culverts and steep grades can push past the high end.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Driveway</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approx. sq ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Resurface existing (rough)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">New with base (rough)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Single car, short</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 x 40 = 400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$400-$1,200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$800-$2,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Single car, typical</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 x 50 = 600</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$600-$1,800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,200-$3,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two car</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 x 40 = 800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$800-$2,400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,600-$4,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Long rural lane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 x 200 = 2,400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2,400-$7,200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4,800-$12,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Very long lane</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 x 500 = 6,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$6,000+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$12,000+</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Long driveways get cheaper per square foot because the fixed costs, getting equipment to the site and setting up, are spread over more area, and suppliers often discount larger orders. They also get more expensive in absolute terms faster than people expect, because a 500-foot lane is a lot of stone. A rural driveway is often the one place where the low install cost of gravel matters most, since paving the same length would be several times the price.
          </p>

          <p>
            The base is what you are really paying for on a new build. A thin layer of pretty stone dumped on topsoil will rut, sink and turn to mud within a season or two, and you will end up paying to build the base later anyway. A proper base, typically several inches of large crushed stone topped with a layer that compacts tightly, is what keeps a gravel driveway firm and makes it cheap to maintain over the long run.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are getting quotes, ask each contractor how deep the finished driveway will be, what stone goes in each layer, whether fabric is included, and whether the price covers excavation and haul-off of topsoil. Two quotes for the same square footage can differ by thousands of dollars simply because one includes a real base and one does not. The cheaper quote is not a bargain if it skips the layers that make the driveway last.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stone-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Type of Gravel Should You Use?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Driveway stone is sold by size and grading, and the names vary by region and by state transportation department, which makes shopping confusing. The key distinction is between clean stone, which is crushed rock in a narrow size range with the fines washed or screened out, and dense-graded stone, which mixes crushed rock with smaller pieces and stone dust so it packs into a hard, nearly solid layer. Most good driveways use both: clean stone for drainage lower down, dense-graded on top.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Stone</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What it is</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best use</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Rough cost</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#3 or similar large stone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean crushed stone, roughly 1 to 2 inches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bottom base layer on soft ground</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower end per ton</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#57</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean crushed stone, roughly 1/2 to 1 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Middle layer, drainage, some top layers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Crusher run</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Crushed stone plus fines and dust (also called crush and run or dense grade)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Top layer or base; compacts hard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often cheapest</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">#411 or similar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smaller stone mixed with stone dust</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smooth, firm top surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pea gravel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small rounded stone, about 3/8 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Paths and patios, not driving</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Recycled concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Crushed old concrete, graded like crusher run</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget base or top layer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often low</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Crusher run is the workhorse. Because the fines fill the gaps between larger pieces, it compacts into a firm surface that resists rutting and does not roll underfoot. It can get dusty in dry weather and muddy in a thaw if the base below does not drain, and it sheds fines over time. #57 stone, by contrast, drains freely and stays clean but does not lock together as tightly, so on its own it can feel loose and shift under tires, especially on slopes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pea gravel is the stone people most often regret. It is attractive and comfortable underfoot, but the round pieces roll against each other like ball bearings, so tires sink and spin, ruts form fast, and the stone migrates into the lawn. If you love the look, use it only inside a plastic stabilizer grid, or limit it to paths. Angular crushed stone is what you want anywhere a car turns, brakes or climbs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Asphalt millings, ground up from old road surfaces, are another budget option that sits between gravel and paving. They compact and partially bind together over time, which makes them less dusty and less prone to rolling than loose stone once they settle. Our guide to an <a href="/asphalt-millings-driveway/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">asphalt millings driveway</a> covers the costs and the drawbacks if you are weighing the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Much Gravel Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The math is simple. Multiply length by width to get square feet, multiply by the depth in feet to get cubic feet, and divide by 27 to get cubic yards. Crushed stone typically weighs somewhere around 1.3 to 1.5 tons per cubic yard depending on the material and how tightly it is packed, so multiply cubic yards by about 1.4 to estimate tons. Most suppliers sell by the ton, some by the yard, and either can convert for you if you give them the area and depth.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE FORMULA</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Length x width x depth in feet / 27 = cubic yards; x 1.4 = tons</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 12-by-50-foot driveway with 4 inches of new top stone is 600 x 0.33 / 27 = about 7.4 cubic yards, or roughly 10 tons. Order about 10 percent extra for compaction and edges.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Depth depends on the job. A refresh over a sound base usually needs 2 to 3 inches of top stone. A new driveway on firm, well-drained soil is commonly built 6 to 8 inches deep in total. On soft clay or wet ground, 10 to 12 inches of layered stone over fabric is not unusual. A common layered build is roughly 4 inches of large base stone, 4 inches of #57 or similar, and 2 to 4 inches of crusher run or a similar dense-graded top.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is a worked example for a new 12-by-50-foot driveway built 10 inches deep. That is 600 square feet times 0.83 feet, or about 500 cubic feet, which is roughly 18.5 cubic yards and somewhere around 26 tons of stone. At $35 per ton, the stone alone is roughly $900, plus delivery for two or three truckloads. Add excavation, fabric, spreading and compaction, and a total around $2,000 to $3,000 is typical in many areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not order by eye. A dump truck load looks enormous on the ground and spreads thin across a driveway. Underordering means a second delivery charge; overordering by a lot means a pile you have to move. Measure carefully, include any turnaround or parking pad, and tell the supplier exactly what you are building so they can suggest the right material for your area.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Drives the Price Up?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Excavation and soil. Building a durable gravel driveway usually means stripping topsoil and organic material, which compresses and holds water, down to firmer subsoil. That dirt has to go somewhere, and hauling it away costs money. Clay soil that stays wet may need deeper excavation, more base stone, and a woven geotextile stabilization fabric, which typically adds somewhere around 10 to 50 cents per square foot but keeps the stone from pushing down into the mud.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grading and drainage. Water is the enemy of every driveway, and gravel is no exception. A well-built gravel driveway has a crown, a slight high point in the middle, so water sheds to the sides. Road maintenance guides for gravel roads commonly cite a crown of about 4 percent, roughly half an inch per foot. Where the driveway crosses a ditch, a culvert pipe may be required, often with a permit from the town or county. Ditches, swales and a culvert can add more to the bill than the stone itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Edging. Gravel wants to spread into the lawn, especially where plows and tires push it. Edging holds the stone in place and gives a cleaner look. Options range from buried timbers and steel or aluminum landscape edging at the low end to paver borders or concrete curbs at the high end, and prices commonly run from a few dollars to $15 or more per linear foot installed. On a long rural lane, many owners skip edging and simply accept some spread.
          </p>

          <p>
            Access, slope and region. A site that a dump truck cannot reach easily, a steep grade that needs more careful compaction and erosion control, or a location far from a quarry all push the price up. Stone is heavy and freight is a large share of its cost, so a driveway near an active quarry can cost noticeably less than the same driveway an hour away. Local demand matters too: spring and early summer are busy, and contractors are sometimes more flexible on price in the shoulder seasons.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Much Does Gravel Driveway Upkeep Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The ongoing cost is what separates gravel from paving. Stone gets pushed to the edges by tires, carried away by snowplows, and washed out by heavy rain, and potholes form where water sits. Most gravel driveways need raking or regrading once or twice a year and a top-up of an inch or two of fresh stone every two to five years, depending on traffic, slope and climate. A tractor or ATV with a box blade or drag makes regrading a weekend job; hiring it out often costs a few hundred dollars.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget roughly a few hundred dollars every couple of years for fresh stone and grading on a typical residential driveway, more for long lanes or steep grades that wash out. Weed control is another small recurring cost if you use herbicide or a landscape fabric under the top layer. Snow removal is trickier on gravel: set plow shoes or snow blower skids high so the blade rides above the stone, and expect to rake some gravel back out of the lawn in spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            Potholes should be fixed by digging out the soft spot, filling with the same crushed stone, and compacting it, rather than tossing loose stone into a puddle. Most potholes are a drainage problem first. If water pools in the same place every spring, regrade so it runs off, or add a drain. Fixing the cause costs once; filling the same hole every few months costs forever.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing gravel never needs is sealing. There is no binder to protect, so driveway sealer has nothing to do on loose stone. If you later pave over your gravel, though, that changes: asphalt needs a first sealcoat after it has cured, and periodic resealing after that. Our <a href="/best-driveway-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best driveway sealer</a> guide covers that step when you get there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-paving" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is Gravel Cheaper Than Asphalt or Concrete Over Time?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Up front, it is not close. Asphalt typically runs roughly $4 to $10 per square foot installed and plain concrete roughly $8 to $18, against roughly $1 to $5 for gravel. On a 600-square-foot driveway that is a difference of thousands of dollars, and on a long rural lane it can be tens of thousands. See our guides to <a href="/asphalt-driveway-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">asphalt driveway cost</a> and <a href="/concrete-driveway-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete driveway cost</a> for the full breakdowns.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Factor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Gravel</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Asphalt</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Concrete</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Installed cost (rough)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1-$5/sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4-$10/sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$8-$18/sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ongoing upkeep</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Regrade and top up every few years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealcoat every 2-5 years, crack fill</td>
