                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Snow removal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Harder; plow takes stone</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easy</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Easy</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Drainage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Permeable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sheds water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sheds water</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Look</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rustic, informal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smooth black</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Smooth gray or decorative</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DIY friendly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with equipment rental</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mostly no</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Over 20 years, gravel still usually comes out cheapest in total, even with regular top-ups, because paving costs so much to install and asphalt also needs sealing and crack repair. The gap narrows for short, flat driveways where gravel upkeep is light but paving cost is also modest. It widens for long lanes, where gravel is often the only sensible choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost is not the only factor. Gravel is dusty in summer, loose underfoot, hard to shovel, rough for strollers and wheelchairs, and some towns or homeowners associations require a paved apron at the street. Paved driveways look tidier and are easier to clear. If you want a middle ground, tar and chip gives a stone look with a bound surface; see our <a href="/tar-and-chip-vs-asphalt/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">tar and chip vs asphalt</a> comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            One smart path is to build gravel now and pave later. A properly built gravel base is exactly what asphalt or concrete needs underneath, so a well-compacted gravel driveway that has settled for a season or two can often be paved over with less base work. That spreads the cost over time and lets you see how the driveway drains before committing to a permanent surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dumping stone on topsoil</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Topsoil and sod compress and hold water, so stone spread straight on lawn sinks, ruts and turns to mud within a season or two. Strip the organic layer, add fabric on soft ground, and build a compacted base first. It costs more on day one and far less over the life of the driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing pea gravel for a driveway</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rounded pea gravel rolls under tires, ruts quickly and wanders into the lawn. It looks good in photos and frustrates owners within months. Use angular crushed stone such as crusher run or a dense-graded top layer anywhere a car turns, brakes or climbs, and keep pea gravel for paths.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the crown</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A flat gravel driveway holds water, and standing water is what creates potholes and soft spots. Grade a crown so the center sits slightly higher than the edges, roughly half an inch per foot, and make sure the water has somewhere to go at the sides rather than pooling along the edge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not compacting each layer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spreading all the stone at once and driving on it to pack it down leaves soft spots and ruts. Each layer should be compacted with a plate compactor or roller, ideally slightly damp, before the next goes on. Loose, uncompacted stone settles unevenly and needs regrading almost immediately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering by guesswork</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A truckload looks huge in a pile and disappears across a driveway. Calculate cubic yards from length, width and depth, convert to tons at about 1.4 per yard, and add 10 percent. A second delivery fee for a shortfall can cost more than the extra stone would have.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a gravel driveway last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The base can last for decades if it drains well and is not overloaded. The surface is never permanent: stone migrates and wears, so most driveways need a top-up every two to five years and regrading once or twice a year. Think of gravel as a driveway you maintain continuously rather than replace on a schedule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a gravel driveway myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, it is the most DIY-friendly driveway type, but it is heavy work. You will likely need to rent a skid steer or compact track loader and a plate compactor, and have stone delivered by dump truck. Small resurfacing jobs are easily DIY; new builds on soft soil or with culverts are often worth hiring out.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a ton of gravel cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At about 1.4 tons per cubic yard, one ton is roughly 0.7 cubic yards. That covers roughly 110 square feet at 2 inches deep, roughly 75 square feet at 3 inches, or roughly 55 square feet at 4 inches. Exact coverage depends on the stone and how tightly it compacts, so order a little extra.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the cheapest gravel for a driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Crusher run and recycled crushed concrete are often the cheapest per ton and also among the best surfaces because they compact hard. Prices depend heavily on what local quarries and recyclers produce, so ask a nearby supply yard what they sell as a driveway top layer and base stone, and compare delivered prices.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need landscape fabric under gravel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On firm, well-drained soil, many driveways do fine without it. On soft clay or wet ground, a woven geotextile stabilization fabric is worth the modest cost because it keeps base stone from sinking into the soil. Thin landscape fabric for weeds is a different product and will not stabilize a driveway.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you plow a gravel driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but set the plow shoes or snow blower skids high so the blade rides an inch or so above the stone. Plowing early in the season before the ground freezes firmly tends to pull up the most gravel. Expect to rake some stone back from the lawn in spring and top up where it thinned.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a gravel driveway add value to a home?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It adds function more than value. In rural areas gravel is normal and buyers expect it. In suburban neighborhoods, a paved driveway is often expected, and gravel may be seen as a project. A well-built gravel driveway that drains properly is still far better than a worn-out, potholed paved one.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

