        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A gravel driveway typically costs roughly $1 to $3 per square foot to resurface over a sound base and roughly $2 to $5 or more to build new with excavation, fabric and layered stone. Common driveway stone runs roughly $20 to $60 per ton plus delivery, and crushed stone weighs about 1.4 tons per cubic yard, which makes estimating your order straightforward.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The money you save up front goes partly into upkeep: regrading, topping up and fixing drainage. Build a proper base with angular stone, crown it, compact each layer, and gravel will stay firm and cheap for years. If you want a cleaner, low-maintenance surface later, that same base is ready for asphalt or concrete when the budget allows.
          </p>
